menu "CPU Configuration"

choice CHOICE_CPU_ARCHITECTURE
    prompt "CPU architecture"
    rsource "common/arch/**/**/KConfig"
endchoice

choice CHOICE_CPU_CORE
    prompt "CPU core"
    rsource "common/cores/**/**/KConfig"
endchoice

choice CHOICE_CPU_FAMILY
    prompt "CPU family"
    rsource "**/KConfig"
endchoice

choice CHOICE_CPU
    prompt "CPU model"
    rsource "**/**/KConfig"
endchoice

config CONFIG_CPU_CLOCK_FREQUENCY_MHZ
    int "CPU clock frequency in MHz (150-800)"
    default 150
    range 150 800
    depends on CPU_RP2350
    help
      Set the desired RP2350 system clock frequency in MHz.
      The actual frequency is rounded down to the nearest value
      achievable by the PLL (12 MHz XOSC, VCO 750-1600 MHz).
      Values above 150 MHz require overclocking.
      Core voltage is automatically adjusted (with ~10% safety margin):
        <=250 MHz: 1.10V     <=370 MHz: 1.35V     <=480 MHz: 1.70V
        <=324 MHz: 1.25V     <=384 MHz: 1.40V     <=512 MHz: 1.80V
        <=348 MHz: 1.30V     <=424 MHz: 1.50V     <=560 MHz: 1.90V
                             <=452 MHz: 1.60V     >560 MHz:  2.10V

      2.10V is where it stops, for every frequency in this range. The
      regulator's next step is 2.20V with nothing in between, and the core
      is nominally 1.10V. If a clock is unstable, lower the clock.

      DO NOT pick this number for the CPU alone. Flash and PSRAM SCK is
      clk_sys/N for an INTEGER N = ceil(clk_sys / the 133 MHz part limit),
      so raising clk_sys past a divider boundary makes external memory
      SLOWER. Both are on the same QMI bus that XIP code fetches from, so
      that is paid by every instruction, not just by data:

        clk_sys   N   SCK       verdict
        618 MHz   5   123.6     highest stable on pico_plus2; the default
        636 MHz   5   127.2     UNSTABLE on hardware (fails gcc torture)
        660 MHz   5   132.0     best SCK of the N=5 band, untested
        678 MHz   6   113.0     TRAP: asking for 680 loses 9% vs 618,
                                and it HardFaults in flash XIP anyway
        696 MHz   6   116.0     TRAP: asking for 700 loses 6% of memory
        750 MHz   6   125.0
        792 MHz   6   132.0
        798 MHz   6   133.0     exactly 6*133; the best SCK in the range
        804 MHz   7   114.9     TRAP: 6 MHz more costs 14% of memory

      Almost all of that table is arithmetic, not experience, and a good
      divider does not make a frequency reachable. What has actually been
      run on pico_plus2: 618 stable, 636 unstable, 678 HardFaults. 636
      failing at only +2.9% says the ceiling is real and close, so treat
      every row below it as a hypothesis with a known-bad neighbour.

      Requesting 800 yields 798 (VCO 1596, postdiv 2), which is the one
      frequency in the whole range where SCK lands exactly on the part
      limit. There is no reason to ask for anything between 799 and 930.

      Above ~620 MHz the QMI min_deselect field (5 bits, 31 cycles) can no
      longer cover the parts' 50 ns CS-high time, and the programmed
      deselect shrinks as clk_sys rises: 50.2 ns at 618, 47.0 at 660,
      38.8 at 798. 618 was the last in-spec point. This is a field-width
      limit, not a tuning knob.

config CONFIG_CPU_CORE_VOLTAGE_MV
    int "Core voltage override in mV (0 = pick from the frequency)"
    default 0
    depends on CPU_RP2350
    help
      Force the POWMAN core regulator to this voltage instead of the value
      the frequency table in requiredVregCode() would choose. 0 keeps the
      automatic behaviour.

      Exists so the voltage can be swept without editing Zig, because the
      automatic value is almost certainly too high. The >560 MHz band sits
      at 2100 mV, which was established at 618 MHz on the 2026-07-30
      configuration: 2000 mV failed the dijkstra repro ~4 runs in 5 and
      2100 mV passed 5 in 5. Much has changed since (PSRAM 133 MHz, the
      /tmp tmpfs, the pool and logging work), so that floor deserves
      re-testing -- 2100 mV on a core whose nominal supply is 1100 mV is
      not somewhere to sit by default.

      The regulator only offers 1800 / 1900 / 2000 / 2100 mV in this
      region; asking for anything else is a compile error.

      How to test it properly: the failure it guards against is ~80%
      intermittent, so a single clean boot proves nothing. Use the 76-test
      reduced repro (the first 75 ir_tests in sorted order followed by
      mibench_dijkstra.c, ~50 s a run), run each voltage at least 5 times,
      and compare failure RATES. An early wrong conclusion in this area
      came from one lucky pass.

config CONFIG_CPU_HAS_FPU
    bool

config CONFIG_CPU_USE_FPU
    bool "Use floating point unit"
    depends on CONFIG_CPU_HAS_FPU

config CONFIG_CPU_HAS_DCP
    bool
    help
      The CPU has Raspberry Pi's double coprocessor (DCP) on CP4: a set of
      primitives that a short instruction sequence composes into an IEEE double
      operation. Selected by the CPU, not by the user.

      Unlike the FPU, the DCP holds state (X, Y and EFD) that the hardware does
      not stack on exception entry, and a DCP sequence is not atomic. When user
      code may execute those sequences the context switch has to save and
      restore that state itself -- see CONFIG_BUILD_USERSPACE_HARDWARE_FP.


endmenu
